// Test: the order in which `>>` evaluates its juxtaposed arguments, and when
// their effects reach the output relative to their values.
//
// Every other file under output/ checks what `>>` prints. None can see *when*
// it evaluated what, because a value looks the same whenever it was computed.
// An argument that prints while being evaluated is the only way to tell an
// engine that evaluates the whole line first from one that evaluates and emits
// one item at a time — and to tell left-to-right from any other order.
uno() {
    >> "[1]"
    <~ "a"
}

dos() {
    >> "[2]"
    <~ "b"
}

>> uno() dos() ¶
>> "sep" ¶

// The same question with the arguments nested inside an expression, where an
// engine that reorders for a fast path would show it.
>> uno() " y " dos() ¶
